# The durable tier, as the one object the schema job and every silo read — docs/plan/05 § Durable.
#
# Copy this file, fill in real connection strings, and point `bootstrap.sh --shards` at it. It becomes
# the Secret `cybercloud-durable-shards`, which both the schema job and the silo pods consume with
# `envFrom`. ⚠ DO NOT COMMIT THE FILLED-IN COPY. `.gitignore` does not know about it.
#
# ── The key format ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Each key is an environment variable name that the .NET configuration provider maps to a section
# path by turning `__` into `:`. So
#
#   CyberCloud__Storage__Durable__Shards__durable00  →  CyberCloud:Storage:Durable:Shards:durable00
#
# and that is the same section `CyberCloudStorageOptions` binds in a silo. `kubectl create secret
# --from-env-file` splits each line on the FIRST `=`, which is why a connection string full of `=` and
# `;` needs no quoting or escaping here.
#
# ── ⚠ SHARD IDS ARE LOAD-BEARING AND PERMANENT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The id is the shard map's key. docs/plan/05 § The shard map: assignment is "at tenant creation and
# it is permanent", and there is no automatic rebalancing. Renaming `durable00` moves every tenant
# assigned to it to a shard that does not exist. Pick names that survive a datacentre move — not
# hostnames.
#
# ── The shard count ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# docs/plan/05 § Durable starts at "N independent PostgreSQL instances (16 to start)". Three are shown
# below; a real file has `durable00` through `durable15`, plus `platform`. Adding shard 17 later is
# one more line here plus one more run of the schema job — that is the whole of "capacity is added at
# the front, not redistributed at the back".
#
# ── The `platform` shard ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ A SEVENTEENTH SHARD THAT docs/plan/05 DOES NOT MENTION, AND IT IS NOT OPTIONAL. Every platform
# grain in docs/plan/04 § Grain taxonomy — the tenant directory, the shard map, the provider registry
# — is null-tenant, and `Orleans.Multitenant` hands the storage layer the literal string `Null` for
# those. Left unset, `DurableTierOptions.NullTenantShard` hashes that sentinel into the tenant shard
# list: deterministic, arbitrary, and indistinguishable from working right up until somebody adds a
# shard and the tenant directory moves to a different server. `charts/platform` sets
# `CyberCloud:Storage:Durable:NullTenantShard` to `platform`; this file is where the shard itself is
# declared.

CyberCloud__Storage__Durable__Shards__durable00=Host=pg-durable-00.example.internal;Port=6432;Database=cybercloud;Username=cybercloud;Password=CHANGE-ME;Application Name=cybercloud-durable00;SSL Mode=VerifyFull
CyberCloud__Storage__Durable__Shards__durable01=Host=pg-durable-01.example.internal;Port=6432;Database=cybercloud;Username=cybercloud;Password=CHANGE-ME;Application Name=cybercloud-durable01;SSL Mode=VerifyFull
CyberCloud__Storage__Durable__Shards__platform=Host=pg-platform.example.internal;Port=6432;Database=cybercloud;Username=cybercloud;Password=CHANGE-ME;Application Name=cybercloud-platform;SSL Mode=VerifyFull

# ── Two things NOT to put in these strings ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `Maximum Pool Size` — `ConfiguredShardConnections.Canonicalise` overrides it from
# `CyberCloud:Storage:Durable:MaxPoolSize`, which docs/plan/05 § Storage provider wiring fixes at 5
# and whose arithmetic (30 silos × 16 shards × 20 = 9 600) makes the default fatal. A value here is
# silently replaced, which is worse than being rejected.
#
# `Max Auto Prepare` and `No Reset On Close` — set from
# `CyberCloud:Storage:Durable:PgBouncerTransactionMode` for the same reason. Port 6432 above is
# PgBouncer, and docs/plan/05 § Storage provider wiring calls transaction-mode pooling
# "non-negotiable"; the flag that goes with it is chart configuration, not a connection-string field.
#
# `Application Name` IS worth setting per shard, and is shown above: the connection budget in
# docs/plan/05 § Storage provider wiring is counted per shard, and `application_name` is what makes
# `pg_stat_activity` able to count it.
